 at  Prince Albert's Pine Grove Correctional  Center for more than five months  awaiting trial  but for almost 10 weeks she's also been  on a hunger strike  right now and myself there's nothing but  cold water the fence blow freezing cold  air we're locked right I we just got off  a 21 hour walk up they're all single  cells and we only get checked on once an  hour but Eagle says it's not just  conditions here motivating her protest  at a national level indigenous people  continue to be over represented in  Canada's prison system in his most  recent report the federal Corrections  investigator found that the total  indigenous offender population has  increased by 40 percent in the last  decade and despite accounting for just  five percent of the total adult  population indigenous people make up 32  percent of the prison population among  women that number is more than 50  percent there's been six indigenous men  who have committed suicide  this year alone  and we're not even done here yet that's  serious stuff Advocates say prisons are  just not equipped to deal with the  intergenerational trauma that's followed  many decades of abuse at Canada's  residential schools I know people and  one of them is my son he's 32 years old  he's in there for crystal meth and uh  when he gets thrown into a cell there's  no supports for mental health there's no  support for addictions and I feel this  happens to a lot of indigenous people  the Saskatchewan government says it's  monitoring the hunger strike at the Pine  Grove Correctional Center and while the  federal Corrections investigator has  been making recommendations to improve  the situation in federal prisons in his  most recent report he found very little  has been done it's why Faith Eagles says  she will back down  incarcerated that are mistreated that  aren't treated like humans vowing to  continue this act of protest until  conditions for indigenous prisoners  improve Heather your ex West Global News  Calgary  Special O